{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And His anger burned, etc., for he is going.</b> The verse is precise in saying <b>he</b>, to give the reason why HaShem's anger burned against him: he did not say to Balak's officers in the morning that HaShem had given him permission to go, but rather ״he rose early in the morning and saddled, etc.״ and ״he went with the officers, etc.״ — which indicates that his going was of his own desire and he did not require permission from on High. And that is what the verse means by <b>for he is going</b> — meaning, he is going of his own volition without permission from HaShem; therefore His anger burned against him." "Further, it appears according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Zohar Ḥadash 169:) who said concerning the verse ״וַיֵּלֶךְ שֶׁפִי״ that it is the way of the forces of impurity that rest upon a person to seek isolation alone, and here when HaShem saw that <b>he is going</b> — meaning, he alone was separated from Balak's officers and doing his deed — this indicates that he was preparing his steps to carry out his accursed desire. And although his two young men were with him, they did not prevent his isolation, for they were accustomed to him and he would do his deed before them, and HaShem knew his evil way and His anger burned against him. Further, the verse may intend by saying <b>he is going</b> that HaShem knew that this wicked man's desire to go had overwhelmed him of his own accord; even had Balak not sent after him, he was going of his own volition, and in this he revealed the depth of his hatred for the people of HaShem — and therefore His anger, may He be blessed, burned against him." "<b>And his two young men were with him.</b> One must understand for what purpose the verse informs us that his two young men were with him. Our Sages of blessed memory (Tanchuma) said: to teach proper conduct, etc. Yet it remains difficult: why did the verse not inform us of this until after mentioning HaShem's anger, may He be blessed, and the going forth of the angel as an adversary to him? It may be explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 43:) that to three people the harmful spirit is not seen, and all the more so does not harm them. Here, since there went out against Balaam an adversary — a harmful angel — Scripture informs us of the reason that it did not harm him and was not even revealed to him: because he and his two young men were with him — behold, they are three."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:43.505559636Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}